<section id="faq" class="home-deferred home-faq" aria-labelledby="home-faq-title">
    <div class="home-faq-container home-faq-grid">
        <header class="home-faq-intro">
            <span class="home-faq-eyebrow">Getting started</span>
            <h2 id="home-faq-title">Frequently asked <span>questions.</span></h2>
            <p>The essentials about setup, devices, public endpoints and pricing. Open a question for a quick answer.</p>
            <a href="/documents" class="home-faq-guide">Explore the documentation <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
            <div><a href="/faq" class="home-faq-guide">See all questions <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
        </header>
        <div class="home-faq-list">
                <details class="home-faq-item" open="open">
                    <summary>
                        <span>Do I need port forwarding or a public IP?</span>
                        <span class="home-faq-toggle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="home-faq-answer">
                        <p>No. Localtonet creates an outbound connection from your device and gives your local service a public endpoint. It works behind NAT or CGNAT without changing your router&#x27;s port forwarding settings.</p>
                        <a href="/documents">See how to set up a tunnel <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                    </div>
                </details>
                <details class="home-faq-item">
                    <summary>
                        <span>Can I connect multiple devices to one account?</span>
                        <span class="home-faq-toggle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="home-faq-answer">
                        <p>Yes. Create a separate token for each device in your dashboard, then use that token in the Localtonet app on that device. You can manage each device and its tunnels separately.</p>
                        <a href="/dashboard">Open your dashboard <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                    </div>
                </details>
                <details class="home-faq-item">
                    <summary>
                        <span>Can I keep the same public address for my tunnel?</span>
                        <span class="home-faq-toggle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="home-faq-answer">
                        <p>Yes. Reserve a domain or port so your tunnel keeps the same public endpoint when it reconnects. You can also connect your own domain to a web tunnel. Configure these options in your dashboard.</p>
                        <a href="/dashboard">Manage your tunnels <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                    </div>
                </details>
                <details class="home-faq-item">
                    <summary>
                        <span>How do Balance and Pay-as-you-go work?</span>
                        <span class="home-faq-toggle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="home-faq-answer">
                        <p>Add credit to your Balance to use paid features. Paid tunnels are billed hourly, and their usage cost is deducted from that credit. You can view your remaining Balance and usage history in your account, then top up when needed.</p>
                        <a href="/blog/introducing-pay-as-you-go-pricing-for-localtonet">Read the Pay-as-you-go guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                    </div>
                </details>
                <details class="home-faq-item">
                    <summary>
                        <span>Can I open a terminal on my own machine from the browser?</span>
                        <span class="home-faq-toggle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="home-faq-answer">
                        <p>Yes. Run localtonet --terminal enable --authtoken YOUR_TOKEN on the machine and approve the short code in your dashboard. A Terminal button then opens a real shell in your browser. The device only makes outbound connections, so there is no inbound port to open and no SSH server to install. Opening a shell requires two-factor approval. Localtonet keeps a record of each connection (when and from which IP address), but it does not store the commands you type or their output.</p>
                        <a href="/devices">Open My Devices <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                    </div>
                </details>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

